---Confidence in a validation verdict, on the producer's native 0-1 scale. Deliberately unconstrained in the model: the smithy-rs server SDK rejects `@range` on a double (smithy-rs#2007). The bound is enforced where the value is produced instead — Control Plane projects this from stored content and omits the member when the stored value falls outside 0-1, so an out-of-range producer value never sur...
---@alias aws.continuum.ValidationConfidence number

---How strong the evidence behind an exploitability verdict is, weakest first. Modeled as named tiers rather than the producer's 1-4 ordinal: the number alone carries no meaning outside Continuum's own table, and a customer branching on "was this actually run" needs the distinction by name. The declaration order is the ordering.
---@alias aws.continuum.ValidationConfidenceTier "SOURCE_READ"|"STATIC_PROVEN"|"BUILD_PROVEN"|"RUNTIME_PROVEN"

---Projected content for a VALIDATION_EVIDENCE artifact: the validation agent's durable proof of exploitability.
---@class aws.continuum.ValidationEvidenceBody
---@field exploitable aws.continuum.ExploitabilityVerdict Exploitability verdict. Explicit UNCERTAIN replaces the producer's tri-state `exploitable: true | false | null`, so an absent verdict is never confused with a negative one.
---@field terminalStatus? aws.continuum.ValidationTerminalStatus How the validation run ended. Absent when the producer reported a status with no mapping yet: the rest of the body still projects, so one unmapped enum never costs a customer the whole artifact.
---@field flowVerdicts? aws.continuum.FlowVerdicts One verdict per attack flow assessed in the bundle.
---@field attackFlowsConfirmed? aws.continuum.AttackFlowIds Identifiers of attack flows upgraded to Confirmed provenance.
---@field exploitArtifact? aws.continuum.ValidationExploitArtifact Concrete payload and its observed effect, when validation produced one by sending an HTTP request. Only an HTTP proof projects here. A validation agent that proves exploitability by RUNNING code against the repository projects `executedProof`, and one that proves it by reading the source projects `sourceAnalysis`. All three are mutually exclusive: at most one is present.
---@field executedProof? aws.continuum.ValidationExecutedProof Proof the agent produced by executing code against the repository under test, with the output that execution produced.
---@field sourceAnalysis? aws.continuum.ValidationSourceAnalysis Proof the agent produced by reading the repository's source, with nothing built or run.
---@field perFindingResults? aws.continuum.PerFindingResults Per-finding breakdown for a multi-finding bundle. Absent for a single-finding bundle.

---@class aws.continuum.ValidationException
---@field message string
---@field fieldList? aws.continuum.ValidationExceptionFieldList

---@class aws.continuum.ValidationExceptionField
---@field path string
---@field message string

---@alias aws.continuum.ValidationExceptionFieldList aws.continuum.ValidationExceptionField[]

---Proof of exploitability the validation agent produced by executing code against the repository under test, with the output that execution produced.
---@class aws.continuum.ValidationExecutedProof
---@field code aws.continuum.ValidationProofCode The code that was run. Not a request payload: it imports the repository's own modules and drives the vulnerable code path directly.
---@field output? aws.continuum.ValidationProofOutput What the execution printed, including the agent's own PASS/FAIL assertions. Absent when the run printed nothing retained.
---@field observedEffect? aws.continuum.ExploitEffect Effect the execution actually produced. `observedEffect` rather than `effect`: this one was witnessed, and the name has to stay distinguishable from `ValidationSourceAnalysis.inferredEffect`, which was not.

---A concrete exploit payload and the effect validation observed from it.
---@class aws.continuum.ValidationExploitArtifact
---@field payload aws.continuum.ArtifactText The exact payload sent.
---@field targetUrl aws.continuum.ArtifactCitation Target the payload was sent to.
---@field httpMethod aws.continuum.ArtifactHttpMethod HTTP method the payload was sent with.
---@field headers? aws.continuum.ArtifactHeaders Headers sent with the payload.
---@field responseStatus? number HTTP status observed in the response.
---@field responseSnippet? aws.continuum.ArtifactProofBody Leading portion of the response body, bounded by the producer.
---@field effect aws.continuum.ExploitEffect Effect observed from sending the payload.

---The executed proof's program. Further members, such as command, workingDirectory and supportingInputs, can be added later as optional details.
---@class aws.continuum.ValidationProofCode
---@field proofCode aws.continuum.ValidationProofMaterials Proof program files, main file first, in their retained order.

---The retained merged console output; its structure allows optional additions.
---@alias aws.continuum.ValidationProofConsoleOutput string

---One proof program file. Further members, such as path and purpose, can be added later as optional details.
---@class aws.continuum.ValidationProofMaterial
---@field content aws.continuum.ValidationProofMaterialText The file's non-blank text, exactly as retained.

---The retained proof file text; its structure allows optional additions.
---@alias aws.continuum.ValidationProofMaterialText string

---The proof program's files, bounded so later optional details can be added.
---@alias aws.continuum.ValidationProofMaterials aws.continuum.ValidationProofMaterial[]

---Retained run output. Further members, such as completion and outputCompleteness, can be added later as optional details.
---@class aws.continuum.ValidationProofOutput
---@field consoleOutput aws.continuum.ValidationProofConsoleOutput What the run printed: standard output and standard error merged, as retained. The text may be empty.

---The Markdown analysis summary; its structure allows optional additions.
---@alias aws.continuum.ValidationProofSummary string

---Proof of exploitability the validation agent produced by reading the repository's source, with nothing built or run. Further members, such as diagram and references, can be added later as optional details.
---@class aws.continuum.ValidationSourceAnalysis
---@field summary aws.continuum.ValidationProofSummary The non-blank analysis as Markdown: the annotated entry-to-sink walk, its citations, and why the checked guards are absent or bypassed.
---@field inferredEffect? aws.continuum.ExploitEffect Effect the agent concluded WOULD occur. Deliberately not `effect`: nothing was executed, so this is reasoned from the source and must not be read as an observation. See `ValidationExecutedProof.observedEffect`.

---How a validation run ended. Producer statuses map onto these; an unmapped status omits `terminalStatus` while the rest of the body still projects.
